here the numbers with three ones cannot be captured by any other interval.\n   - These unique \\( m \\) values are found to be of the form \\( m = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} + 1 \\) for some integer \\( n \\geq 1 \\). This form is derived from the pattern observed in examples and the structure of numbers with three ones in binary.\n\n### Final Answers\n(1) \\boxed{\\text{For every positive integer } m, \\text{ there exists at least one positive integer } k \\text{ such that } f(k) = m}\n\n(2) \\boxed{\\text{All positive integers } m \\text{ of the form } m = \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} + 1 \\text{ for some integer } n \\geq 1}", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet \\( S \\) represent the set of positive integers that have exactly 3 ones in their binary representation. We will analyze the function \\( f(k) \\), defined as the number of elements in the set \\(\\{k+1, k+2, \\dots, 2k\\}\\) that belong to \\( S \\).\n\n1. **Expression for \\( f(k+1) \\):**\n   \\[\n   f(k+1) = \n   \\begin{cases}\n   f(k), & \\text{if } 2k+1 \\notin S, \\\\\n   f(k) + 1, & \\text{if } 2k+1 \\in S.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   This is due to the fact that adding one to \\( k \\) shifts the range of interest to \\(\\{k+2, k+3, \\dots, 2k+1, 2k+2\\}\\). If \\( 2k+1 \\) is in \\( S \\), it increases the count by 1; otherwise, the count remains the same.\n\n2. **Base Cases:**\n   \\[\n   f(1) = 0, \\quad f(2) = 0.\n   \\]\n\n#### Analysis for Powers of 2\n\nConsider when \\( k = 2^s \\). Then:\n\\[\nf(2^s) = \\text{number of elements in } \\{2^s + 1, 2^s + 2, \\ldots, 2^{s+1}\\} \\text{ belonging to } S.\n\\]\n\nIn binary representation:\n\\[\n2^s + 1 = 1 \\underbrace{0 \\cdots 01}_{s-1}, \\quad 2^{s+1} = 1 \\underbrace{0 \\cdots 0}_{s+1}.\n\\]\n\nThere are \\(\\binom{s}{2}\\) ways to choose 2 spots from \\(s\\) available positions (excluding the first and last for placing ones), so:\n\\[\nf(2^s) = \\binom{s}{2} = \\frac{s(s-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nAs \\( s \\) increases, clearly \\(\\frac{s(s-1)}{2}\\) becomes unbounded. This establishes that \\( f(k) \\) is unbounded.\n\n3. **Proof of Unbounded \\( f(k) \\):**\n   Since \\( f(1) = 0, f(2) = 0 \\), and \\( \\boxed{f(k) = \\frac{s(s-1)}{2} } \\) results as \\( k \\) approaches powers of 2, \\( f(k) \\) being unbounded is confirmed. As \\( k \\) scans all positive integers, \\( f(k) \\) covers all non-negative integers.\n\n\\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\nNext, we determine the specific positive integer \\(k\\) for each \\( m \\) to satisfy \\( f(k)=m \\):\n\nConsidering \\( m = \\frac{s(s-1)}{2} + 1 \\):\n\\[\nf(k) = m \\implies \\frac{s(s-1)}{2} + 1 = m.\n\\]\nFor integer \\( k \\) given \\( k = 2^s + 2 \\):\n\\[\nf(2^s + 2) = \\frac{s(s-1)}{2} + 1.\n\\]\n\nGiven \\( 2k = 2(2^s + 2) = 2^{s+1} + 2^2 \\), binary representation:\n\\[\n2^s + 2 + 1 = 10\\ldots011 \\quad (\\text{with } s-2 \\text{ zeros})\n\\]\n\\[\n2k + 1 = 2^{s+1} + 2+1.\n\\]\n\nThese configurations validate:\n\\[\nf(2^s + 2 - 1) = f(2^s + 2) - 1, \\quad f(2^s + 2 + 1) = f(2^s + 2) + 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, confirming \\( f(k) = \\frac{s(s-1)}{2} + 1 \\) has unique solution \\( k = 2^s + 2 \\) for each integer \\( s \\ge 2 \\):\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=2^s + 2 \\text{ if } f(k) = \\frac{s(s-1)}{2} + 1}\n\\]\n\\(\\blacksquare\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "For a positive integer \\( n \\), let \\( f(n) \\) be the number of perfect squares \\( d \\) such that \\( |n - d| \\leq 250 \\). Find all \\( n \\) such that \\( f(n) = 15 \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find all positive integers n such that the number of perfect squares d satisfying |n - d| \u2264 250 is exactly 15. Hmm, let me try to unpack this step by step.\n\nFirst, let's understand the function f(n). For a given n, f(n) counts how many perfect squares d are within 250 units away from n. In other words, the distance between n and d is at most 250. So, if I imagine the number line, n is somewhere, and we look at the interval from