fore, gluing the 63rd square (leaving a1) is valid. Then Player A glues the 63rd square, and Player B has no moves, so Player B loses. Therefore, the answer is Player A wins.\n\nBut this seems too straightforward. Is there a catch?\n\nWait, what if the knight's graph is not 2-connected? Suppose there are some squares whose removal would disconnect the graph. Then those squares cannot be glued until the end. But as per our earlier analysis, the knight's graph is 2-connected, so no such squares exist. Therefore, every square except a1 can be glued in any order, and the game will proceed until all 63 are glued. Therefore, Player A wins.\n\nHowever, I recall that in some knight's graphs, there might be articulation points. Let me think of a specific example. Suppose we remove square b1. From a1, the knight can move to b3 and c2. If b3 is glued, then from a1, you can go to c2. If c2 is glued, then from a1, you can't move. But in the problem, players are gluing squares one by one, and after each glue, the remaining squares must be reachable. So if a player glues b3, the remaining squares must still be reachable from a1. But after gluing b3, can the knight still reach all other squares? Yes, because from a1, it can go to c2, and from c2, it can reach other squares. Similarly, gluing c2 would still allow the knight to go to b3. Therefore, removing b3 or c2 doesn't disconnect the graph. Hence, no articulation points.\n\nAnother example: suppose we glue d4. Can the knight still reach all squares? Yes, because there are multiple paths around d4.\n\nTherefore, the knight's graph is indeed 2-connected, so no single square removal disconnects it. Therefore, all 63 squares can be glued in any order, and Player A wins.\n\nBut wait, let's consider the endgame. When there are two squares left: a1 and one other. The next player can glue that other square, leaving only a1. Then the following player can't move. So if there are two squares left (a1 and x), the player whose turn it is can