he constraint that after each move, the knight on a1 must still be able to reach all remaining unglued squares. The key steps to determine the winner are as follows:\n\n1. **Knight's Graph Connectivity**: The chessboard's knight graph (where squares are nodes connected by knight moves) is **2-connected**. This means no single square's removal disconnects the graph. Thus, any square can be glued without violating the connectivity condition, provided the remaining squares still form a connected component accessible from a1.\n\n2. **Total Moves**: Since the knight starts on a1, this square cannot be glued (as doing so would trap the knight). Therefore, there are **63 squares** available to glue. Each turn reduces the count by one, and the game ends when no valid moves remain.\n\n3. **Parity Argument**: With 63 squares to glue, the number of moves is **odd**. Player A (the first player) will make the 1st, 3rd, ..., 63rd moves, while Player B makes the 2nd, 4th, ..., 62nd moves. Player A thus makes the final move, leaving Player B unable to act.\n\n**Conclusion**: Under optimal play, Player A can always force a win by leveraging the 2-connectedness of the knight's graph and the odd number of available moves. \n\n**Answer**: \\boxed{Player\\ A}", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\n8.49 In an international chessboard's square \\( a1 \\), a white knight is placed. Two players take turns using glue to paint one square of the chessboard, one square each. Once the paint is applied, the knight must be able to jump to any of the unpainted squares without getting stuck. According to international chess rules, knight jumps in an L-shape move. Player A starts first. The first player unable to comply with the game requirements loses. Determine who will win under optimal play strategies.\n\n\n1. **Initial Conditions and Rules Understanding**:\n    - The chessboard is composed of 64 squares.\n    - The knight starts at \\(a1\\).\n    - Player A and Player B take turns painting one square at a time.\n    - The knight must always be able to move to any unpainted square.\n    - Player A starts the game.\n\n2. **Strategic Considerations**:\n    - Since Player A starts and there are a total of 64 squares, Player A will paint on the 1st, 3rd, ... , 63rd moves.\n    - Player B will paint on the 2nd, 4th, ... , 62nd, and the last 64th move.\n    - The key to understanding the problem is identifying that the game will end on the 64th move, which will be made by Player B.\n\n3. **Gameplay Outcome**:\n    - The knight's ability to jump dictates that there might be specific squares that, if painted, will force the knight not to be able to continue its move.\n    - Player A only needs to ensure that there will always be a valid square for the knight to move, thus forcing Player B to eventually run out of moves.\n\n4. **Final Move Analysis**:\n    - With an optimal strategy assumed and proper gameplay, the turn when the board is full without valid unpainted squares will be Player B\u2019s turn.\n    - Therefore, the last move (64th square) is painted by Player B.\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n    - As the last move leading to no valid knight moves must be made by Player B, it practices that Player B will lose the game.\n\nThus, \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Player A wins}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Show that if \\( n > 2 \\) is an integer, and \\(\\alpha = \\frac{\\pi}{n}\\), then\n\n\\[ \n\\sin \\alpha \\sin 2 \\alpha + \\sin 2 \\alpha \\sin 3 \\alpha + \\cdots + \\sin (n-2) \\alpha \\sin (n-1) \\alpha = \\frac{n}{2} \\cos \\alpha \n\\]", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove this trigonometric identity involving the sum of products of sines with arguments in arithmetic progression. The sum is from k=1 to k=n-2 of sin(k\u03b1)sin((k+1)\u03b1), where \u03b1 = \u03c0/n and n is an integer greater than 2. The right-hand side is supposed to be (n/2)cos\u03b1. Hmm, let's see how to approach this.\n\nFirst, I remember that there are product-to-sum formulas for trigonometric functions. Maybe I can use those to simplify each term in the sum. The product of sines can be expressed as a difference of cosines. The formula is:\n\nsin A sin B = [cos(A - B) - cos(A + B)] / 2\n\nSo if I apply this to each term sin(k\u03b1)sin((k+1)\u03b1), let's set A = k\u03b1 and B = (k+1)\u03b1. Then:\n\nsin(k\u03b1)sin((k+1)\u03b1) = [cos(k\u03b1 - (k+1)\u03b1) - cos(k\u03b1 + (k+1)\u03b1)] / 2\n= [cos(-\u03b1) - cos((2k+1)\u03b1)] / 2\n= [cos\u03b1 - cos((2k+1)\u03b1)] / 2\n\nSince cosine is even, cos(-\u03b1) = cos\u03b1. So each term in the sum becomes (cos\u03b1 - cos((2k+1)\u03b1))/2. Therefore, the entire sum is the sum from k=1 to n-2 of [cos\u03b1 - cos((2k+1)\u03b1)] / 2.\n\nLet me write that out:\n\nSum = (1/2) * [ \u03a3_{k=1}^{n-2} cos\u03b1 - \u03a3_{k=1}^{n-2} cos((2k+1)\u03b1) ]\n\nFirst, let's compute the first sum \u03a3_{k=1}^{n-2} cos\u03b1. Since cos\u03b1 is a constant with respect to k, this is just (n-2)cos\u03b1.\n\nSo the first part is (n-2)cos\u03b1 / 2.\n\nNow, the second sum is \u03a3_{k=1}^{n-2} cos((2k+1)\u03b1). Let's see if we can compute this sum. Let's write out the terms to see if there's a telescoping pattern or something.\n\nFirst, when k=1: cos(3\u03b1)\nk=2: cos(5\u03b1)\n...\nk=n-2: cos((2(n-2)+1)\u03b1) = cos((2n - 4 +1)\u03b1) = cos((2n -3)\u03b1)\n\nBut since \u03b1 = \u03c0/n, substituting that in, the term becomes cos((2n -3)\u03c0/n) = cos(2\u03c0 - 3\u03c0/n) = cos(3\u03c0/n) because cos(2\u03c0 - x) = cosx. Wait, cos(2\u03c0 - x) = cosx. So cos((2n -3)\u03c0/n) = cos(2\u03c0 - 3\u03c0/n) = cos(3\u03c0/n). Hmm, but 2n -3 over n is 2 - 3/n, so 2\u03c0 - 3\u03c0/n. Yes, that's correct.\n\nWait, but is that helpful? Let's see. Let me write out the terms of the second sum:\n\n\u03a3_{k=1}^{n-2} cos((2k+1)\u03b1) = cos3\u03b1 + cos5\u03b1 + ... + cos((2n -3)\u03b1)\n\nBut since \u03b1 = \u03c0/n, this becomes:\n\ncos(3\u03c0/n) + cos(5\u03c0/n) + ... + cos((2n -3)\u03c0/n)\n\nHmm, note that (2n -3)\u03c0/n = 2\u03c0 - 3\u03c0/n, which is equivalent to cos(3\u03c0/n) because cosine is even and periodic with period 2\u03c0. Wait, actually, cos(2\u03c0 - x) = cosx, so cos((2n -3)\u03c0/n) = cos(3\u03c0/n). Wait, but that would mean that the last term is the same as the first term? That can't be unless n is specific.\n\nWait, let's check for a small n. Let's take n=4. Then \u03b1 = \u03c0/4.\n\nThe sum would be from k=1 to 2 (since n-2=2):\n\ncos3\u03b1 = cos(3\u03c0/4) and cos5\u03b1 = cos(5\u03c0/4). But when k=2, 2k+1 = 5, so 5\u03b1 = 5\u03c0/4, but 2n -3 when n=4 is 5, so 5\u03c0/4. However, if n=4, the sum would be cos(3\u03c0/4) + cos(5\u03c0/4) = -\u221a2/2 + (-\u221a2/2) = -\u221a2. But according to the original formula, the total sum should be (4/2)cos(\u03c0/4) = 2*(\u221a2/2) = \u221a2. But the left-hand side would be [ (4-2)/2 cos(\u03c0/4) - (sum of cos terms)/2 ] = [ (2)(\u221a2/2) - (-\u221a2)/2 ] = [ \u221a2 + \u221a2/2 ] = (3\u221a2)/2, which doesn't match. Wait, maybe my substitution is wrong. Wait, hold on.\n\nWait, let's test n=4. The original sum is sin(\u03c0/4)sin(2\u03c0/4) + sin(2\u03c0/4)sin(3\u03c0/4). Let's compute each term:\n\nFirst term: sin(\u03c0/4)sin(\u03c0/2) = (\u221a2/2)(1) = \u221a2/2\n\nSecond term: sin(\u03c0/2)sin(3\u03c0/4) = (1)(\u221a2/2) = \u221a2/2\n\nSum: \u221a2/2 + \u221a2/2 = \u221a2\n\nRight-hand side: (4/2)cos(\u03c0/4) = 2*(\u221a2/2) = \u221a2. So it works. But according to the previous breakdown:\n\nSum = [(n-2)/2 cos\u03b1 - (sum of cos terms)/2] = [(2)/2 * \u221a2/2 - (sum)/2]\n\nWait, sum of cos terms is cos3\u03c0/4 + cos5\u03c0/4 = -\u221a2/2 + (-\u221a2/2) = -\u221a2\n\nSo [(2/2)(\u221a2/2) - (-\u221a2)/2] = (\u221a2/2 + \u221a2/2) = \u221a2, which matches. So that works. So even though in this case, the sum of the cos terms is negative, but when subtracted, it becomes addition.\n\nBut in this case, when n=4, the sum of cos terms is two terms: cos3\u03b1 and cos5\u03b1, which are equivalent to cos(3\u03c0/4) and cos(5\u03c0/4). But 5\u03c0/4 is equivalent to \u03c0 + \u03c0/4, which is -cos(\u03c0/4). So maybe there's a pattern here.\n\nBut how to compute the sum \u03a3_{k=1}^{n-2} cos((2k+1)\u03b1) where \u03b1 = \u03c0/n.\n\nWait, note that the arguments of the cosine are (2k+1)\u03c0/n for k from 1 to n-2. Let's consider k from 0 to n-1: (2k+1)\u03c0/n would then be an odd multiple of \u03c0/n from 1\u03c0/n to (2n-1)\u03c0/n. However, in our case, k starts from 1, so the terms are from 3\u03c0/n to (2n-3)\u03c0/n. So we have cos(3\u03c0/n) + cos(5\u03c0/n) + ... + cos((2n -3)\u03c0/n). Notice that (2n -3)\u03c0/n = 2\u03c0 - 3\u03c0/n, and since cosine is even and periodic, cos(2\u03c0 - 3\u03c0/n) = cos(3\u03c0/n). So the sum is symmetric around \u03c0. Wait, so the terms from 3\u03c0/n to (2n -3)\u03c0/n are symmetric with respect to \u03c0. Let's check for n=5.\n\nn=5, \u03b1=\u03c0/5. The sum is cos(3\u03c0/5) + cos(5\u03c0/5) + cos(7\u03c0/5). Wait, k=1:3\u03c0/5, k=2:5\u03c0/5=\u03c0, k=3:7\u03c0/5. But n-2=3 when n=5, so sum from k=1 to 3. cos(3\u03c0/5) + cos(\u03c0) + cos(7\u03c0/5). But cos(7\u03c0/5) = cos(2\u03c0 - 3\u03c0/5) = cos(3\u03c0/5). So the sum becomes 2cos(3\u03c0/5) + cos\u03c0. Which is 2cos(3\u03c0/5) -1. But how does that help?\n\nAlternatively, maybe we can pair terms. For example, in n=5, the sum is cos(3\u03c0/5) + cos\u03c0 + cos(7\u03c0/5) = cos(3\u03c0/5) + cos(\u03c0) + cos(2\u03c0 - 3\u03c0/5) = cos(3\u03c0/5) + (-1) + cos(3\u03c0/5) = 2cos(3\u03c0/5) -1. So in general, if n is odd, the terms might pair up, leaving some middle term. If n is even, similar pairing.\n\nBut maybe there is a general formula for the sum of cosines in arithmetic progression. Let me recall that.\n\nThe sum of cos(k\u03b8) from k=1 to m is [sin(m\u03b8/2) / sin(\u03b8/2)] * cos((m+1)\u03b8/2). But in our case, the angles are not k\u03b8 but (2k+1)\u03b1. Let's see.\n\nAlternatively, the sum \u03a3_{k=0}^{m-1} cos(a + kd) can be written as [sin(md/2)/sin(d/2)] * cos(a + (m-1)d/2). This is the formula for the sum of cosines in arithmetic progression.\n\nIn our case, the sum is \u03a3_{k=1}^{n-2} cos((2k+1)\u03b1). Let's adjust the index to start from k=0.\n\nLet me set k' = k -1. Then when k=1, k'=0; when k=n-2, k' = n-3. So the sum becomes \u03a3_{k'=0}^{n-3} cos((2(k'+1)+1)\u03b1) = \u03a3_{k'=0}^{n-3} cos((2k' +3)\u03b1). So this is a sum from k'=0 to n-3 of cos(3\u03b1 + 2\u03b1k'). So this is an arithmetic progression with initial term a = 3\u03b1, common difference d = 2\u03b1, and number of terms m = n-2.\n\nApplying the formula:\n\nSum = [sin(md/2)/sin(d/2)] * cos(a + (m -1)d/2)\n\nSubstituting:\n\nm = n -2\n\nd = 2\u03b1\n\na = 3\u03b1\n\nTherefore,\n\nSum = [sin((n -2)(2\u03b1)/2)/sin(2\u03b1/2)] * cos(3\u03b1 + (n -3)2\u03b1/2)\n\nSimplify:\n\n= [sin((n -2)\u03b1)/sin\u03b1] * cos(3\u03b1 + (n -3)\u03b1)\n\n= [sin((n -2)\u03b1)/sin\u03b1] * cos(3\u03b1 + n\u03b1 -3\u03b1)\n\n= [sin((n -2)\u03b1)/sin\u03b1] * cos(n\u03b1)\n\nBut since \u03b1 = \u03c0/n,\n\nn\u03b1 = \u03c0, so cos(n\u03b1) = cos\u03c0 = -1\n\nSo the sum becomes:\n\n[sin((n -2)\u03c0/n)/sin(\u03c0/n)] * (-1)\n\nsin((n -2)\u03c0/n) = sin(\u03c0 - 2\u03c0/n) = sin(2\u03c0/n) because sin(\u03c0 - x) = sinx\n\nTherefore,\n\nSum = [sin(2\u03c0/n)/sin(\u03c0/n)] * (-1)\n\nWe can simplify sin(2\u03c0/n) as 2sin(\u03c0/n)cos(\u03c0/n), so:\n\nSum = [2sin(\u03c0/n)cos(\u03c0/n)/sin(\u03c0/n)] * (-1) = 2cos(\u03c0/n)*(-1) = -2cos\u03b1\n\nTherefore, going back to the original expression:\n\nSum = (n -2)cos\u03b1 / 2 - [ -2cos\u03b1 ] / 2\n\nWait, the total Sum was:\n\nSum = (1/2)[(n -2)cos\u03b1 - \u03a3 cos terms]\n\nBut the sum of cos terms was computed as -2cos\u03b1, so:\n\nSum = (1/2)[(n -2)cos\u03b1 - (-2cos\u03b1)] = (1/2)[(n -2)cos\u03b1 + 2cos\u03b1] = (1/2)[n cos\u03b1] = (n/2) cos\u03b1\n\nWhich is exactly what we needed to prove. So that works out. Therefore, the identity holds.\n\nLet me verify this with another example. Let's take n=3. Then \u03b1=\u03c0/3.\n\nThe left-hand side sum is from k=1 to 1 (since n-2=1) term: sin(\u03c0/3)sin(2\u03c0/3). Compute this:\n\nsin(\u03c0/3) = \u221a3/2, sin(2\u03c0/3)=\u221a3/2. Product is (\u221a3/2)^2 = 3/4.\n\nRight-hand side: (3/2)cos(\u03c0/3) = (3/2)(1/2) = 3/4. So it matches.\n\nAnother example, n=5.\n\nLeft-hand side: sum from k=1 to 3 of sin(k\u03c0/5)sin((k+1)\u03c0/5)\n\nCompute each term:\n\nk=1: sin(\u03c0/5)sin(2\u03c0/5)\n\nk=2: sin(2\u03c0/5)sin(3\u03c0/5)\n\nk=3: sin(3\u03c0/5)sin(4\u03c0/5)\n\nEach term:\n\nFirst term: sin(\u03c0/5)sin(2\u03c0/5) \u2248 0.5878 * 0.9511 \u2248 0.559\n\nSecond term: sin(2\u03c0/5)sin(3\u03c0/5) \u2248 0.9511 * 0.9511 \u2248 0.9045\n\nThird term: sin(3\u03c0/5)sin(4\u03c0/5) \u2248 0.9511 * 0.5878 \u2248 0.559\n\nSum \u2248 0.559 + 0.9045 + 0.559 \u2248 2.022\n\nRight-hand side: (5/2)cos(\u03c0/5) \u2248 2.5 * 0.8090 \u2248 2.0225. Close enough, considering rounding errors. So it works.\n\nTherefore, the proof using the product-to-sum formula and summing the resulting cosine series by using the arithmetic progression sum formula leads to the desired identity.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove the identity\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\alpha \\sin 2\\alpha + \\sin 2\\alpha \\sin 3\\alpha + \\cdots + \\sin (n-2)\\alpha \\sin (n-1)\\alpha = \\frac{n}{2} \\cos \\alpha\n\\]\n\nfor \\( n > 2 \\) and \\(\\alpha = \\frac{\\pi}{n}\\), follow these steps:\n\n1. **Apply the Product-to-Sum Formula**:\n   For each term \\(\\sin k\\alpha \\sin (k+1)\\alpha\\), use the identity:\n   \\[\n   \\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(A - B) - \\cos(A + B)]\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\(A = k\\alpha\\) and \\(B = (k+1)\\alpha\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sin k\\alpha \\sin (k+1)\\alpha = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos(-\\alpha) - \\cos((2k+1)\\alpha)] = \\frac{1}{2}[\\cos\\alpha - \\cos((2k+1)\\alpha)]\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sum the Transformed Terms**:\n   The entire sum becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} \\cos\\alpha - \\frac{1}{2} \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} \\cos((2k+1)\\alpha)\n   \\]\n   The first sum is straightforward:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2}(n-2)\\cos\\alpha\n   \\]\n\n3. **Evaluate the Second Sum**:\n   The second sum involves cosines of angles in arithmetic progression:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} \\cos((2k+1)\\alpha) = \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} \\cos\\left(\\frac{(2k+1)\\pi}{n}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Recognize this as an arithmetic sequence with initial term \\(3\\alpha\\), common difference \\(2\\alpha\\), and \\(n-2\\) terms. Using the formula for the sum of cosines in arithmetic progression:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=0}^{m-1} \\cos(a + kd) = \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{md}{2}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{d}{2}\\right)} \\cos\\left(a + \\frac{(m-1)d}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(a = 3\\alpha\\), \\(d = 2\\alpha\\), \\(m = n-2\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} \\cos((2k+1)\\alpha) = \\frac{\\sin((n-2)\\alpha)}{\\sin\\alpha} \\cos(n\\alpha)\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\alpha = \\frac{\\pi}{n}\\), \\(n\\alpha = \\pi\\), and \\(\\cos(n\\alpha) = -1\\). Simplify using \\(\\sin((n-2)\\alpha) = \\sin\\left(\\pi - \\frac{2\\pi}{n}\\right) = \\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{n}\\right)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{k=1}^{n-2} \\cos((2k+1)\\alpha) = \\frac{\\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{n}\\right)}{\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{n}\\right)} (-1) = -2\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{n}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. **Combine the Results**:\n   Substitute back into the original sum:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2}(n-2)\\cos\\alpha - \\frac{1}{2}(-2\\cos\\alpha) = \\frac{n}{2}\\cos\\alpha\n   \\]\n\n**Final Answer**:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{n}{2} \\cos \\alpha}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Introduce the problem**: We need to prove that for an integer $n > 2$ and $\\alpha = \\frac{\\pi}{n}$, the following sum holds:\n\n    \\[\n    \\sin \\alpha \\sin 2\\alpha + \\sin 2\\alpha \\sin 3\\alpha + \\cdots + \\sin (n-2)\\alpha \\sin (n-1)\\alpha = \\frac{n}{2} \\cos \\alpha\n    \\]\n\n2. **Geometric interpretation**: Consider a regular $n$-gon inscribed in a unit circle, with vertices labeled as $A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_n$ and center $O$.\n\n3. **First method to calculate the area**: Divide the $n$-gon into $n$ isosceles triangles by connecting $O$ to each vertex $A_i$. The area of each triangle $A_iOA_{i+1}$ is given by:\n\n    \\[\n    T_{A_iOA_{i+1}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{n} = \\frac{1}{2} \\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{n}\n    \\]\n\n    Therefore, the total area $T$ of the $n$-gon is:\n\n    \\[\n    T = n \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\sin \\frac{2\\pi}{n} = n \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2\\alpha = \\frac{n}{2} \\sin 2\\alpha = n \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\alpha\n    \\]\n\n4. **Second method to calculate the area**: Divide the $n$-gon into $n-2$ triangles by drawing diagonals from $A_1$ to every other vertex $A_i$. Each triangle $A_1A_iA_{i+1}$ (for $i = 2, 3, \\ldots, n-1$) has an area given by:\n\n    \\[\n    T_{A_1A_iA_{i+1}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot A_1A_i \\cdot A_1A_{i+1} \\cdot \\sin \\alpha\n    \\]\n\n    The length $A_1A_i$ can be calculated using the inscribed angles and properties of the unit circle. For $i \\leq \\frac{n}{2}$, $A_1A_i = 2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{(i-1)\\pi}{n}\\right)$. For $i > \\frac{n}{2}$, $A_1A_i = 2 \\sin \\left(\\pi - \\frac{(n-i+1)\\pi}{n}\\right) = 2 \\sin \\left(\\frac{(i-1)\\pi}{n}\\right)$, thus:\n\n    \\[\n    A_1A_i = 2 \\sin ((i-1) \\alpha)\n    \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the total area using the second method**: Using the previously obtained lengths, we sum the areas of the triangles:\n\n    \\[\n    T = \\sum_{i=2}^{n-1} \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\left(2 \\sin ((i-1)\\alpha)\\right) \\cdot \\left(2 \\sin (i\\alpha)\\right) \\cdot \\sin \\alpha\n    = \\sum_{i=2}^{n-1} 2 \\sin ((i-1)\\alpha) \\sin (i\\alpha) \\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{2}\n    \\]\n\n    Simplifying the sum:\n\n    \\[\n    T = \\sum_{i=2}^{n-1} \\sin ((i-1)\\alpha) \\sin (i\\alpha) \\sin \\alpha\n    \\]\n\n6. **Equating the areas**: From both methods, the total area $T$ of t