(C/5 - 2) | \u2264 0.05*( (9/5)C + 32 )\n\nFirst, maybe simplify the right-hand side. Let's compute 0.05*(9/5 C + 32). Let's convert 0.05 to a fraction: 0.05 = 1/20. So:\n\n(1/20)*(9/5 C + 32) = (9/100)C + (32/20) = (9/100)C + 1.6\n\nSo the inequality becomes:\n\n| (C/5 - 2) | \u2264 (9/100)C + 1.6\n\nNow, to solve this absolute value inequality, we can split it into two cases.\n\nCase 1: (C/5 - 2) \u2265 0  \nCase 2: (C/5 - 2) < 0\n\nLet's handle Case 1 first:  \nIf C/5 - 2 \u2265 0, then C/5 \u2265 2 \u21d2 C \u2265 10.\n\nSo in this case, |C/5 - 2| = C/5 - 2. The inequality becomes:\n\nC/5 - 2 \u2264 (9/100)C + 1.6\n\nMultiply all terms by 100 to eliminate denominators:\n\n100*(C/5) - 100*2 \u2264 100*(9/100)C + 100*1.6  \n20C - 200 \u2264 9C + 160  \n20C - 9C \u2264 160 + 200  \n11C \u2264 360  \nC \u2264 360/11 \u2248 32.727...\n\nBut remember in Case 1, C \u2265 10. So the solution here is 10 \u2264 C \u2264 32.727...\n\nNow, Case 2: C/5 - 2 < 0 \u21d2 C < 10.\n\nThen |C/5 - 2| = -(C/5 - 2) = -C/5 + 2.\n\nSo the inequality becomes:\n\n- C/5 + 2 \u2264 (9/100)C + 1.6\n\nMultiply all terms by 100 to eliminate denominators:\n\n-20C + 200 \u2264 9C + 160  \n-20C -9C \u2264 160 - 200  \n-29C \u2264 -40  \nMultiply both sides by -1 (remember to reverse the inequality):  \n29C \u2265 40  \nC \u2265 40/29 \u2248 1.379...\n\nBut in Case 2, C < 10. So the solution here is approximately 1.379... \u2264 C < 10.\n\nCombining both cases, the overall solution is approximately 1.379 \u2264 C \u2264 32.727.\n\nBut we need to check if these values indeed satisfy the original inequality. Let me verify with some test points.\n\nFor example, at C = 10:  \nF_approx = 2*10 + 30 = 50  \nF_exact = (9/5)*10 + 32 = 18 + 32 = 50  \nSo deviation is 0, which is \u2264 5% of 50 (which is 2.5). So 0 \u2264 2.5, which is true.\n\nAt C \u2248 32.727:  \nCompute the deviation. Let's calculate F_approx and F_exact.\n\nF_approx = 2*32.727 + 30 \u2248 65.454 + 30 = 95.454  \nF_exact = (9/5)*32.727 + 32 \u2248 58.9086 + 32 = 90.9086  \nDeviation: |95.454 - 90.9086| \u2248 4.5454  \n5% of F_exact is 0.05*90.9086 \u2248 4.5454. So the deviation is exactly 5% here. So 32.727 is included.\n\nAt C \u2248 1.379:  \nF_approx = 2*1.379 + 30 \u2248 2.758 + 30 = 32.758  \nF_exact = (9/5)*1.379 + 32 \u2248 2.4822 + 32 = 34.4822  \nDeviation: |32.758 - 34.4822| \u2248 1.7242  \n5% of F_exact is 0.05*34.4822 \u2248 1.7241. So deviation is approximately equal to 5%, which is acceptable. So 1.379 is included.\n\nNow, what happens outside these ranges? Let's check C = 0:  \nF_approx = 0 + 30 = 30  \nF_exact = 0 + 32 = 32  \nDeviation: 2. 5% of 32 is 1.6. So 2 > 1.6, so deviation exceeds 5%. So C=0 is outside.\n\nAt C = 35:  \nF_approx = 70 + 30 = 100  \nF_exact = 63 + 32 = 95  \nDeviation: 5. 5% of 95 is 4.75. So 5 > 4.75, exceeds 5%.\n\nSo the range seems to be approximately 1.379 to 32.727 degrees Celsius.\n\nBut the problem says \"indicate the range of temperatures (in degrees Celsius)\". So we need to present the exact values. Let's recall that 360/11 is exactly 32.727..., and 40/29 is approximately 1.379. Let's write them as fractions.\n\nSo the lower bound is 40/29 \u2248 1.3793, upper bound is 360/11 \u2248 32.7273.\n\nAlternatively, we can write them as exact fractions:\n\n40/29 = 1 and 11/29  \n360/11 = 32 and 8/11\n\nBut maybe the problem expects decimal places? Let's check the instructions. It says to \"indicate the range\" but doesn't specify. Since the original problem is in degrees Celsius, and tourists use this approximation, maybe a practical range would be in whole numbers? But the question says \"range of temperatures\", so it's probably acceptable to leave it as exact fractions or decimals.\n\nAlternatively, if we need exact values, 40/29 and 360/11. Let's confirm if these are correct.\n\nFrom Case 1:\n\n11C \u2264 360 \u21d2 C \u2264 360/11  \nFrom Case 2:\n\n29C \u2265 40 \u21d2 C \u2265 40/29\n\nYes. So the exact bounds are C \u2265 40/29 and C \u2264 3