\u03c0/2, t = tan(\u03c0/4) = 1.\n\nSo, J becomes:\n\n\u222b\u2080\u00b9 [1 / (1 + (2t/(1 + t\u00b2)) + ((1 - t\u00b2)/(1 + t\u00b2)))] * [2 dt/(1 + t\u00b2)]\n\nSimplify the denominator:\n\n1 + [2t + 1 - t\u00b2]/(1 + t\u00b2) = [ (1 + t\u00b2) + 2t + 1 - t\u00b2 ] / (1 + t\u00b2) = [1 + t\u00b2 + 2t + 1 - t\u00b2]/(1 + t\u00b2) = (2 + 2t)/(1 + t\u00b2) = 2(1 + t)/(1 + t\u00b2)\n\nTherefore, the integrand becomes:\n\n1 / [2(1 + t)/(1 + t\u00b2)] * [2 dt/(1 + t\u00b2)] = [ (1 + t\u00b2)/(2(1 + t)) ] * [2/(1 + t\u00b2) ) ] dt = [1/(1 + t)] dt\n\nTherefore, J = \u222b\u2080\u00b9 [1/(1 + t)] dt = ln|1 + t| from 0 to 1 = ln(2) - ln(1) = ln 2\n\nSo J = ln 2\n\nTherefore, going back to the earlier equation:\n\n2I = \u03c0/2 + ln 2 => I = \u03c0/4 + (ln 2)/2\n\nTherefore, the original integral is:\n\n\u03c0/2 - I = \u03c0/2 - [\u03c0/4 + (ln 2)/2] = \u03c0/4 - (ln 2)/2\n\nHence, the value of the integral is \u03c0/4 - (ln 2)/2\n\nWait, let me check this again step by step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\nWe had the original integral = \u03c0/2 - I, where I = \u222b (1 + sin x)/(denominator) dx. Then, using substitution, we found that I = \u03c0/4 + (ln 2)/2. Therefore, original integral is \u03c0/2 - \u03c0/4 - (ln 2)/2 = \u03c0/4 - (ln 2)/2. Yes.\n\nAlternatively, let's verify the substitution for J:\n\nWe set t = tan(x/2), then computed the integrand and ended up with 1/(1 + t) dt from 0 to 1, which is indeed ln 2. So that's correct.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \u03c0/4 - (1/2) ln 2.\n\nAlternatively, we can write it as (\u03c0 - 2 ln 2)/4.\n\nBut both forms are correct. Let me check numerically.\n\nCompute \u03c0/4 \u2248 0.7854, ln 2 \u2248 0.6931, so (0.7854 - 0.3466) \u2248 0.4388. Let me compute the integral numerically to check.\n\nTake x from 0 to \u03c0/2, function f(x) = cos x / (1 + sin x + cos x). Let's approximate.\n\nAt x = 0: cos 0 = 1, denominator = 1 + 0 + 1 = 2, so f(0) = 1/2 = 0.5\n\nAt x = \u03c0/2: cos(\u03c0/2) = 0, so f(\u03c0/2) = 0.\n\nIn the middle, x = \u03c0/4: cos(\u03c0/4) = \u221a2/2 \u2248 0.707, sin(\u03c0/4) = \u221a2/2. Denominator: 1 + \u221a2/2 + \u221a2/2 = 1 + \u221a2 \u2248 2.414. So f(\u03c0/4) \u2248 0.707 / 2.414 \u2248 0.293.\n\nSo the function starts at 0.5, goes to ~0.293 at \u03c0/4, then to 0. The integral should be approximately the average of these values times \u03c0/2, but rough estimate maybe around 0.4*(\u03c0/2) \u2248 0.628, but our analytical result is \u22480.4388. Wait, this discrepancy is concerning. Maybe my analytical result is wrong.\n\nWait, but wait. Wait, when I did substitution for J, I might have made a mistake. Let me check again.\n\nCompute J = \u222b\u2080^{\u03c0/2} 1/(1 + sin x + cos x) dx.\n\nUsing substitution t = tan(x/2):\n\nsin x = 2t/(1 + t\u00b2), cos x = (1 - t\u00b2)/(1 + t\u00b2), dx = 2 dt/(1 + t\u00b2)\n\nDenominator: 1 + [2t/(1 + t\u00b2)] + [(1 - t\u00b2)/(1 + t\u00b2)] = [ (1 + t\u00b2) + 2t + 1 - t\u00b2 ] / (1 + t\u00b2 ) = (2 + 2t)/(1 + t\u00b2) = 2(1 + t)/(1 + t\u00b2)\n\nThus, integrand becomes 1 / [2(1 + t)/(1 + t\u00b2)] * [2/(1 + t\u00b2)] dt = [ (1 + t\u00b2)/(2(1 + t)) ] * [2/(1 + t\u00b2) ] dt = [1/(1 + t)] dt. Correct.\n\nSo integrating from t=0 to t=1 gives ln(2). So J = ln 2. So that part is correct.\n\nThen, we had:\n\nOriginal integral = \u03c0/2 - I, and I = \u222b (1 + sin x)/(1 + sin x + cos x) dx from 0 to \u03c0/2.\n\nUsing symmetry substitution, showed that I = \u222b (1 + cos x)/(denominator) dx, hence 2I = \u222b [ (1 + sin x) + (1 + cos x) ] / denominator dx = \u222b (2 + sin x + cos x)/denominator dx\n\nThen, numerator 2 + sin x + cos x = (1 + sin x + cos x) + 1, so numerator = denominator + 1. Therefore,\n\n2I = \u222b [denominator + 1]/denominator dx = \u222b 1 dx + \u222b 1/denominator dx = \u03c0/2 + J = \u03c0/2 + ln 2\n\nThus, 2I = \u03c0/2 + ln 2 => I = \u03c0/4 + (ln 2)/2\n\nTherefore, original integral = \u03c0/2 - I = \u03c0/2 - \u03c0/4 - (ln 2)/2 = \u03c0/4 - (ln 2)/2 \u2248 0.7854 - 0.3466 \u2248 0.