al of:\n\\[\n2 + 4 + 2 = 8\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "ABC is an acute-angled triangle. The incircle touches BC at K. The altitude AD has midpoint M. The line KM meets the incircle again at N. Show that the circumcircle of BCN touches the incircle of ABC at N.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this geometry problem here. Let me try to parse it step by step. The problem states:\n\n\"ABC is an acute-angled triangle. The incircle touches BC at K. The altitude AD has midpoint M. The line KM meets the incircle again at N. Show that the circumcircle of BCN touches the incircle of ABC at N.\"\n\nHmm, let's break this down. We have an acute-angled triangle ABC. The incircle touches BC at point K. Then there's an altitude AD, and M is the midpoint of AD. The line KM intersects the incircle again at N. We need to show that the circumcircle of triangle BCN is tangent to the incircle of ABC at point N.\n\nFirst, let me visualize the triangle. Since ABC is acute-angled, all altitudes are inside the triangle. AD is the altitude from A to BC, so D is the foot of the altitude on BC. The midpoint M of AD is halfway between A and D.\n\nThe incircle touches BC at K. In a triangle, the point where the incircle touches a side can be determined using the formula: if the sides are of lengths a, b, c opposite to A, B, C respectively, then the touch point on BC is at a distance of (b + c - a)/2 from B. So, if BC is of length a, then BK = (AB + BC - AC)/2. Wait, no, more accurately, in standard notation, with BC = a, AC = b, AB = c, the touch point on BC is at length s - b from B, where s is the semiperimeter. So, BK = s - b, and KC = s - c. Maybe that will come into play.\n\nSo, K is the touch point on BC. Then we connect K to M, the midpoint of the altitude AD, and this line KM meets the incircle again at N. Our goal is to show that the circumcircle of BCN is tangent to the incircle