{m/s}^2 \\times (60 \\, \\text{s})^2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplifying the height equation:**\n   \\[\n   y = 6000 + 36000 - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 3600 = 6000 + 36000 - 18000 = 24000 \\, \\text{m} = 24 \\, \\text{km}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the maximum height reached by the rocket is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{24 \\, \\text{km}}\n\\]\n\n#### Part (b): Checking if the rocket can be a threat to an object at 20 km altitude\n\nSince the maximum height reached by the rocket is 24 km, which is greater than 20 km, the rocket can indeed pose a threat to an object located at an altitude of 20 km.\n\n#### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Thus, part (a) is } \\boxed{24 \\, \\text{km}} \\text{ and part (b) is } \\boxed{\\text{Yes}}.\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given a pyramid $ABCD$. It is known that:\n\n$ADB = DBC$\n\n$ABD = BDC$\n\n$BAD = ABC$.\n\nFind the surface area of the pyramid (the sum of the areas of the four triangles) if the area of the triangle $ABC$ is $10 \\, \\text{cm}^2$.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out this pyramid problem. Hmm, so we have a pyramid ABCD. The base is triangle ABC, and the apex is D. The given conditions are:\n\n1. Angle ADB equals angle DBC.\n2. Angle ABD equals angle BDC.\n3. Angle BAD equals angle ABC.\n\nWe need to find the surface area of the pyramid, given that the area of triangle ABC is 10 cm\u00b2.\n\nFirst, let me visualize the pyramid. ABCD is a triangular pyramid (tetrahedron) with base ABC and apex D. The problem gives three angle equalities. Since all these angles are related to the edges involving points A, B, D, and C, maybe there's some symmetry or congruent triangles here.\n\nLet me note down the given equalities:\n\n- \u2220ADB = \u2220DBC (angle at D in triangle ADB equals angle at B in triangle DBC)\n- \u2220ABD = \u2220BDC (angle at B in triangle ABD equals angle at D in triangle BDC)\n- \u2220BAD = \u2220A