u6570 with $d = 0$, and $A_1$ denote the set of $P$ \u7c7b\u6570 with $d \\neq 0$.\n   \n2. For any four-digit number $\\overline{abcd} \\in A_1$, consider its reverse number $\\overline{dcba}$.\n\n    - Given that $a > b$, $b < c$, and $c > d \\geqslant 1$, it follows that $\\overline{dcba} \\in B$.\n    - Conversely, each $\\overline{dcba} \\in B$ corresponds uniquely to an element $\\overline{abcd} \\in A_1$.\n\n3. Thus, there is a one-to-one correspondence between $A_1$ and $B$, leading to the equality $|A_1| = |B|$.\n\n4. Consequently, $N(P) - N(Q) = |A| - |B| = \\left|A_0\\right| + \\left|A_1\\right| - |B| = \\left|A_0\\right|$.\n\n5. To find $|A_0|$, we count the number of such $P$ \u7c7b\u6570 $\\overline{abc0}$ in $A_0$:\n\n    - $b$ can take values from 0 to 9 (inclusive).\n    - For each $b$, $a$ must satisfy $b < a \\leqslant 9$ and $c$ must satisfy $b < c \\leqslant 9$.\n\n6. Therefore, $a$ and $c$ have $(9 - b)$ possible values each. Hence, for each $b$:\n   \\[\n   (9 - b)^2 \\text{ combinations.}\n   \\]\n\n7. Summing up all possibilities:\n   \\[\n   |A_0| = \\sum_{b = 0}^{9} (9 - b)^2.\n   \\]\n\n8. Calculating the sum:\n   \\[\n   |A_0| = \\sum_{k = 1}^{9} k^2 = \\frac{9 \\times 10 \\times 19}{6} = 285.\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nTherefore, the value of $N(P) - N(Q)$ is:\n\\[ \n\\boxed{285}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( C(n) \\) be the number of distinct prime divisors of \\( n \\). (For example, \\( C(10)=2 \\), \\( C(11)=1 \\), \\( C(12)=2 \\).)\n\nIs the number of such pairs of natural numbers \\( (a, b) \\) finite or infinite, such that \\( a \\neq b \\) and \\( C(a+b) = C(a) + C(b) \\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out whether there are infinitely many pairs of natural numbers (a, b) where a is not equal to b, and the number of distinct prime divisors of their sum, C(a+b), is equal to the sum of the number of distinct prime divisors of a