f areas of similar triangles is equal to the square of the ratio of their corresponding sides. Thus, the similarity ratio between triangles \\(AKL\\) and \\(BKC\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{S}}{\\sqrt{p}}\\right)^2 = \\frac{S}{p}\n   \\]\n\n5. Similarly, the similarity ratio between triangles \\(AKL\\) and \\(DCL\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{S}}{\\sqrt{q}} \\right)^2 = \\frac{S}{q}\n   \\]\n\n6. We know that:\n   \\[\n   S = q \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{S}}{\\sqrt{q} - \\sqrt{S}} \\right)^2\n   \\]\n\n7. From this similarity:\n   \\[\n   q = (\\sqrt{p} - \\sqrt{S})^2\n   \\]\n\n8. Solving for \\(\\sqrt{S}\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{S} = \\sqrt{p} - \\sqrt{q}\n   \\]\n\n9. The total area of the parallelogram \\(S_{\\text{ABCD}}\\) is the area of the triangles summed:\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{ABCD}} = p - S + q\n   \\]\n\n10. Substituting the value of \\(S\\), we get:\n    \\[\n    S_{\\text{ABCD}} = p - (\\sqrt{p} - \\sqrt{q})^2 + q\n    \\]\n\n11. Simplifying further:\n    \\[\n    S_{\\text{ABCD}} = p - (p - 2\\sqrt{pq} + q) + q = 2\\sqrt{pq}\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2 \\sqrt{pq}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "On a \\(2012 \\times 2012\\) board, some cells on the top-right to bottom-left diagonal are marked. None of the marked cells is in a corner. Integers are written in each cell of this board in the following way. All the numbers in the cells along the upper and the left sides of the board are 1's. All the numbers in the marked cells are 0's. Each of the other cells contains a number that is equal to the sum of its upper neighbor and its left neighbor. Prove that the number in the bottom right corner is not divisible by 2011.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to work through this problem step by step. So, we have a 2012x2012 board. The top-right to bottom-left diagonal has some marked cells, none of which are in the corners. All the 