1}^{12} a_i \\right)\n   \\]\n5. Since the total number of red points is 48, we substitute and simplify:\n   \\[\n   m = \\frac{1}{2} \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{12} a_i^2 - 48 \\right)\n   \\]\n6. Using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality on the sums of \\(a_i\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{12} a_i \\right)^2 & \\leq 12 \\sum_{i=1}^{12} a_i^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad 48^2 \\leq 12 \\sum_{i=1}^{12} a_i^2 \\\\\n   \\Rightarrow \\sum_{i=1}^{12} a_i^2 & \\geq \\frac{2304}{12} = 192\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n7. Now, substituting back into our expression for \\(m\\):\n   \\[\n   m \\geq \\frac{1}{2} (192 - 48) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 144 = 72 \n   \\]\n8. Thus, there are at least 72 pairs of red points in the same column. Each pair of these points defines a vertical line in a rectangle. Consequently, in every column, there are combinations of pairs that balance across the horizontal lines. Since there are only \\(\\binom{12}{2} = 66\\) possible pairs of horizontal line segments, by the pigeonhole principle, there must be at least two pairs of red points forming a rectangle.\n9. Therefore, there exists a rectangle where all four vertices are red.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{There exists a rectangle with all vertices colored the same.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "An n-gon has all angles equal and the lengths of consecutive sides satisfy \\(a_1 \\geq a_2 \\geq ... \\geq a_n\\). Prove that all the sides are equal.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to prove that if an n-gon has all angles equal and the lengths of consecutive sides satisfy a\u2081 \u2265 a\u2082 \u2265 ... \u2265 a\u2099, then all the sides must be equal. Hmm, okay. Let's start by recalling what kind of polygons have all angles equal. Regular polygons do, but this isn't necessarily regular since the sides might not be equal. However, the problem states that the sides are in a non-increasing order. 