hat the given equation equates the sum to 1, so:\n   \\[\n   1 \\geq 3\\sqrt[3]{3^{x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - 2x - 2y - 2z}}\n   \\]\n   \n5. Simplify the exponent on the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   3\\sqrt[3]{3^{x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - 2x - 2y - 2z}} = 3^{\\frac{1}{3}(x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - 2x - 2y - 2z) + 1}\n   \\]\n   \n6. To simplify further, let \\(u = x-1\\), \\(v = y-1\\), \\(w = z-1\\). Thus, \\(x = u + 1\\), \\(y = v + 1\\), \\(z = w + 1\\). Then the equation becomes:\n   \\[\n   x^2 + y^2 + z^2 - 2x - 2y - 2z = (u+1)^2 + (v+1)^2 + (w+1)^2 - 2(u+1) - 2(v+1) - 2(w+1)\n   \\]\n   \n7. Simplifying the expanded form:\n   \\[\n   (u^2 + 2u + 1) + (v^2 + 2v + 1) + (w^2 + 2w + 1) - 2u - 2 - 2v - 2 - 2w - 2\n   = u^2 + v^2 + w^2\n   \\]\n   \n8. So, the exponent reduces to:\n   \\[\n   3^{\\frac{1}{3}(u^2 + v^2 + w^2) + 1}\n   \\]\n   \n9. As \\(u, v, w\\) are the shifts of \\(x, y, z\\) by 1, we observe that:\n   \\[\n   1 \\geq 3^{\\frac{1}{3}(u^2 + v^2 + w^2)}\n   \\]\n   Hence,\n   \\[\n   0 \\geq \\frac{1}{3}(u^2 + v^2 + w^2)\n   \\]\n   \n10. This implies:\n    \\[\n    u^2 + v^2 + w^2 = 0\n    \\]\n   \n11. Since \\(u^2 \\geq 0\\), \\(v^2 \\geq 0\\), and \\(w^2 \\geq 0\\) for all real numbers \\(u, v, w\\), the equality \\(u^2 + v^2 + w^2 = 0\\) holds only if \\(u = 0\\), \\(v = 0\\), and \\(w = 0\\).\n   \n12. Therefore, \\(u = 0 \\implies x = 1\\), \\(v = 0 \\implies y = 1\\), \\(w = 0 \\implies z = 1\\).\n   \n13. Hence, the only solution is:\n    \\[\n    (x, y, z) = (1, 1, 1)\n    \\]ulation steps\n  \n14. Conclusion. The unique solution is \\(\\boxed{(1, 1, 1)}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A smart dog could count in the quaternary numeral system. It used the sound \"o\" for zero, \"u\" for one, \"v\" for two, and \"a\" for three. What number does the bark \"\u043e\u0443\u0430\u0432\u043e\u0443\u0430\u0432\" represent in this case?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out what numbe