' - x y = 1.\n\nSo, the differential equation is:\n\n(1 - x^2) y' - x y = 1.\n\nNow, let's verify if the left-hand side series S(x) satisfies this differential equation.\n\nLet S(x) = sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{2n+1}, where a_n = (2n)!! / (2n+1)!!.\n\nCompute (1 - x^2) S'(x) - x S(x):\n\nFirst, compute S'(x) = sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n (2n+1) x^{2n}.\n\nThen, (1 - x^2) S'(x) = sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n (2n+1) x^{2n} - sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n (2n+1) x^{2n+2}.\n\nThe first term is sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n (2n+1) x^{2n}.\n\nThe second term is - sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n (2n+1) x^{2n+2} = - sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1} (2(n-1)+1) x^{2n}.\n\nThen, subtract x S(x): x S(x) = sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{2n+2}.\n\nThus, putting all together:\n\n(1 - x^2) S'(x) - x S(x) = [sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n (2n+1) x^{2n}] - [sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1} (2n - 1) x^{2n}] - [sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{2n+2}].\n\nWait, maybe I made a substitution wrong. Let me reindex the second sum. Let m = n - 1 for the second sum. Then when n=1, m=0: - sum_{m=0}^\\infty a_m (2m +1) x^{2(m+1)} = - sum_{m=0}^\\infty a_m (2m +1) x^{2m + 2}.\n\nSimilarly, the third term is - sum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n x^{2n+2} = - sum_{m=0}^\\infty a_m x^{2m + 2}.\n\nSo combining the second and third terms: - sum_{m=0}^\\infty [a_m (2m +1) + a_m] x^{2m + 2} = - sum_{m=0}^\\infty a_m (2m +2) x^{2m + 2}.\n\nTherefore, the entire expression becomes:\n\nsum_{n=0}^\\infty a_n (2n +1) x^{2n} - sum_{m=0}^\\infty a_m (2m +2) x^{2m +2}.\n\nNow, let's write the first sum as starting from n=0 and the second sum as starting from m=0, which is equivalent to n=1:\n\n= a_0 (1) x^0 + sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n (2n +1) x^{2n} - sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1} (2(n-1) +2) x^{2n}.\n\nWait, the second sum after reindexing: let n = m +1, so m = n -1. Then the second sum is - sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1} (2(n-1) +2) x^{2n} = - sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_{n-1} (2n) x^{2n}.\n\nTherefore, the entire expression is:\n\na_0 (1) + sum_{n=1}^\\infty [a_n (2n +1) - a_{n-1} (2n)] x^{2n}.\n\nNow, set this equal t