e midpoint of the perpendicular from \\( D \\) to the plane \\( A, B, C \\).\n\n3. Similarly, a plane equidistant from these four points can be found where only one of the points is lying on one side of the plane, and the other three points lie on the other side. Thus, there will be four such planes.\n\n4. Now, moving to the second case, assume points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) lie on one side of the plane \\( II \\), equidistant from points \\( A, B, C, \\) and \\( D \\), while points \\( C \\) and \\( D \\) lie on the other side.\n   \n   - Since the plane \\( II \\) is equidistant from points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) on the same side, this plane must be parallel to the line \\( AB \\). Analogously, it must also be parallel to the line \\( CD \\).\n   - Given that points \\( A, B, C, \\) and \\( D \\) do not lie in the same plane, lines \\( AB \\) and \\( CD \\) must be skew.\n   - Then, plane \\( II \\) must pass equidistantly between two parallel planes containing lines \\( AB \\) and \\( CD \\). \n\n5. Consequently, similar to the first set of configurations, there will be three such planes in which two points lie on one side, and the other two points lie on the other side.\n\n6. Adding up all these configurations, the total number of plane solutions is:\n   \\[\n   4 (from\\ first\\ case\\ with\\ three\\ points\\ on\\ one\\ side) + 3 (from\\ second\\ case\\ with\\ two\\ points\\ on\\ each\\ side) = 7 \n   \\]\n   \nThus, the total number of planes equidistant from the given four points in space is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{7}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given seven theorems about measuring angles with arcs [1) central angle, 2) inscribed angle, 3) angle formed by a tangent and a chord at the point of tangency, 4) angle formed by two secants intersecting inside the circle, 5) angle formed by a tangent and a secant not passing through the point of tangency, 6) angle formed by two secants intersecti