he angle between them is half the difference of the intercepted arcs. But since the two tangent points would create two arcs, but since both are tangents, the intercepted arcs would be the major arc and the minor arc between the two points of tangency. The formula is half the difference of the intercepted arcs. But since the two arcs add up to 360 degrees, the difference would be 360 - 2 times the minor arc. Wait, maybe I need to check.\n\nWait, for two tangents, the angle outside is half the difference of the intercepted arcs. But since both intercepted arcs are actually the same as the two points of tangency, but the angle formed is outside the circle. Wait, let's think. If two tangents are drawn from an external point, the angle between them is equal to half the difference of the measures of the intercepted arcs. The intercepted arcs would be the two arcs between the points of tangency. Since the total circle is 360, the difference would be the major arc minus the minor arc. So angle = 1/2 (major arc - minor arc). But since major arc + minor arc = 360, then major arc - minor arc = 360 - 2*minor arc. So angle = 1/2 (360 - 2*minor arc) = 180 - minor arc. Wait, but the standard formula is angle = 1/2 (difference of intercepted arcs). So if the two arcs are, say, 120 and 240, the difference is 120, so angle is 60. That seems right. So angle = 1/2 |arc1 - arc2|, but since one is major and the other is minor, arc1 + arc2 = 360. So the difference would be |arc1 - (360 - arc1)| = |2arc1 - 360|. Then angle is 1/2 of that, so |arc1 - 180|. Hmm, maybe not. Maybe it's better to think that the angle formed by two tangents is half the difference of the intercepted arcs. For example, if the points of tangency are A and B, then the angle at the external point is 1/2 (measure of major arc AB - measure of minor arc AB). So since major arc AB is 360 - minor arc AB, the difference is (360 - minor arc AB) - minor arc AB = 360 - 2*minor arc AB. Then 1/2 of that is 180 - minor arc AB. But that's equivalent to 1/2 (major arc AB - minor arc AB). So angle = 1/2 (major arc - minor arc). So that's the formula for two tangents.\n\nNow, trying to see if all these theorems can be generalized. Let me summarize each theorem's formula:\n\n1) Central angle: angle = arc.\n\n2) Inscribed angle: angle = 1/2 arc.\n\n3) Tangent and chord at tangency: angle = 1/2 arc.\n\n4) Two secants inside: angle = 1/2 (sum of arcs).\n\n5) Tangent and secant outside: angle = 1/2 (difference of arcs).\n\n6) Two secants outside: angle = 1/2 (difference of arcs).\n\n7) Two tangents (circumscribed angle): angle = 1/2 (difference of arcs).\n\nLooking at these, the key seems to be that depending on where the vertex of the angle is located relative to the circle (inside, on, or outside), the measure of the angle relates to either the sum or difference of intercepted arcs, scaled by 1/2. The central angle is a special case where the vertex is at the center, so no scaling. The others (vertex on the circumference, inside, outside) involve 1/2 of sum or difference.\n\nSo perhaps the general formulation is that the measure of an angle formed by two lines intersecting at a point (which could be inside, on, or outside the circle) is equal to half the sum or half the difference of the measures of the intercepted arcs, depending on the location of the vertex. When the vertex is at the center, it's the full arc. When on the circumference (inscribed angle, tangent-chord), it's half the arc. When inside, it's half the sum. When outside, it's half the difference.\n\nSo maybe the general formula is:\n\nIf two lines intersect at a point P and intercept arcs A and B on the circle, then the measure of the angle at P is (1/2)(A \u00b1 B), where the sign depends on the position of P.\n\nBut how to formalize it? Let's see.\n\nFor central angle (vertex at center): angle = A (where A is the intercepted arc). But in the general formula, if we take 1/2 (A + B), but since the two lines are radii, the intercepted arcs would be the same? Wait, no. Wait, for a central angle, there's only one arc intercepted between the two radii. So maybe in that case, the other arc is zero? Not sure. Alternatively, maybe the formula can be written such that when the vertex is at the center, the factor is 1 instead of 1/2, but that complicates the generalization.\n\nAlternatively, think of all angles as involving two arcs, but in some cases, one of the arcs is zero or the entire circle. Wait, maybe not. Let me consider each case:\n\n1) Central angle: Only one arc is involved. The angle equals the measure of that arc. But in terms of two arcs, maybe the two arcs are the same? Or perhaps one arc is the intercepted arc, and the other is zero? Hmm.\n\nWait, perhaps the general formula is that the measure of the angle is half the difference between the measures of the arcs intercepted by the two lines and their respective positions relative to the angle's vertex. Wait, this is getting vague.\n\nLooking for a pattern:\n\n- When the vertex is on the circle (inscribed angle, tangent-chord): angle = 1/2 * intercepted arc.\n\n- When the vertex is inside the circle (two secants): angle = 1/2 * (sum of intercepted arcs).\n\n- When the vertex is outside the circle (two secants, two tangents, or tangent and secant): angle = 1/2 * (difference of intercepted arcs).\n\nCentral angle is a case where the vertex is at the center, and the angle equals the intercepted arc. But this seems like an exception unless we adjust the formula.\n\nWait, perhaps the central angle can be seen as a special case where the \"sum\" of the arcs is twice the arc, and since the angle is half the sum, but that would not align. Wait, if we consider the central angle as a case where the two intercepted arcs are both equal to the arc itself, but that seems forced.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the general formula is:\n\nThe measure of an angle formed by two lines intersecting at point P is equal to half the sum of the measures of the intercepted arcs when P is inside the circle, half the difference when P is outside, and half the measure of a single arc when P is on the circle. The central angle is a special case where P is the center, so perhaps it's considered as half the sum of two arcs that are each equal to the intercepted arc, leading to angle = 1/2 (arc + arc) = arc. But that seems like stretching.\n\nAlternatively, think of the central angle as the case where one of the arcs is the intercepted arc, and the other arc is zero (since the two lines are radii, they don't intercept another arc). Then, the formula could be angle = 1/2 (arc1 + arc2). If arc2 is zero, then angle = 1/2 arc1. But that contradicts because the central angle is equal to the arc, not half. So this approach doesn't fit.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the general formula is:\n\nFor any angle whose vertex is related to a circle, the measure of the angle is equal to half the measure of the arc(s) intercepted by the lines forming the angle, with adjustments based on the position of the vertex.\n\nBut this is too vague.\n\nWait, let's look up if there's a general theorem that covers all these cases. Wait, I recall that in some geometry resources, there's a general angle measure theorem which states that the measure of an angle formed by two intersecting lines is equal to half the difference or sum of the intercepted arcs depending on whether the vertex is outside or inside the circle. Let me try to recall.\n\nYes, the general rule is:\n\n- If the vertex is inside the circle, the angle measure is half the sum of the measures of the intercepted arcs.\n\n- If the vertex is on the circle, the angle measure is half the measure of the intercepted arc.\n\n- If the vertex is outside the circle, the angle measure is half the difference of the measures of the intercepted arcs.\n\nBut wait, the central angle is a case where the vertex is at the center, so it's inside the circle, but according to this rule, it would be half the sum of the intercepted arcs. However, in reality, the central angle is equal to the intercepted arc. So perhaps the general formula needs to account for the position more precisely.\n\nWait, maybe when the vertex is at the center, the two intercepted arcs are actually the same arc, so the sum would be twice the arc, and half of that would be the arc, which matches. Wait, if two lines intersect at the center, each intercepting an arc. Wait, but in the case of a central angle, the two lines are radii intercepting one arc. So maybe the two intercepted arcs are the arc itself and its opposite arc? For example, if the central angle intercepts arc AB, then the other arc intercepted would be arc BA, which is the same as arc AB. Wait, but arcs are directional. Alternatively, maybe in the case of a central angle, the two intercepted arcs are the arc AB and the arc BA, which together make the whole circle, but that doesn't make sense.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps when the vertex is at the center, the two intercepted arcs are the same, so the sum is 2*arc, then half the sum is arc, which is correct.\n\nSimilarly, for an inscribed angle, which is on the circle, the angle is half the intercepted arc. For two secants intersecting inside, the angle is half the sum of the intercepted arcs. For outside, half the difference.\n\nSo putting this all together, the general theorem could be stated as:\n\nThe measure of an angle formed by two lines intersecting at a point P relative to a circle is equal to half the sum of the measures of the intercepted arcs if P is inside the circle, half the difference of the measures of the intercepted arcs if P is outside the circle, and half the measure of a single intercepted arc if P is on the circle.\n\nBut then, how does the central angle fit into this? Because the central angle is formed by two radii, so the vertex is at the center (inside the circle), so according to the general theorem, it should be half the sum of the intercepted arcs. However, we know the central angle is equal to the measure of the arc, not half. So this seems contradictory unless the intercepted arcs in this case are considered to be the same arc twice, making the sum twice the arc, so half the sum would be the arc. For example, if the central angle intercepts arc AB, then the two intercepted arcs would both be arc AB. Therefore, sum is AB + AB = 2AB, half of that is AB, which matches the central angle theorem. But is this a valid interpretation?\n\nBut in reality, when two secants intersect inside the circle, they intercept two different arcs. For example, if two chords intersect inside the circle, they create two pairs of vertical angles, each angle being half the sum of the measures of the intercepted arcs. So in that case, each angle intercepts two distinct arcs. So for the central angle, if we consider that the two intercepted arcs are the same arc (since both sides of the angle are radii), then their sum is twice the arc, leading to half the sum being equal to the arc. So perhaps this is the way to fit the central angle into the general theorem.\n\nTherefore, the general formulation could be:\n\nThe measure of an angle formed by two lines intersecting at a point P with respect to a circle is equal to:\n\n- Half the sum of the measures of the two intercepted arcs when P is inside the circle,\n\n- Half the measure of the single intercepted arc when P is on the circle,\n\n- Half the difference of the measures of the two intercepted arcs when P is outside the circle.\n\nIn this formulation, the central angle theorem (1) is a special case where P is inside the circle (at the center), and the two intercepted arcs are actually the same arc (since both sides are radii), so their sum is twice the arc, and half of that sum is the arc measure, which matches theorem 1.\n\nThe inscribed angle (2) and tangent-chord angle (3) are cases where P is on the circle, so the angle is half the intercepted arc.\n\nThe two secants intersecting inside (4) correspond to P inside, so angle is half the sum.\n\nThe tangent-secant outside (5), two secants outside (6), and two tangents (circumscribed angle) (7) all have P outside, so angle is half the difference of the intercepted arcs.\n\nThis seems to cover all seven cases. Let me verify each one:\n\n1) Central angle: P is inside (at center). The two intercepted arcs are the same arc AB. Sum is AB + AB = 2AB. Half the sum is AB. Correct.\n\n2) Inscribed angle: P is on the circle. Intercepted arc is AB. Angle is 1/2 AB. Correct.\n\n3) Tangent and chord at tangency: P is on the circle. The tangent and chord intercept an arc AB (from the chord's other endpoint to the point of tangency). Angle is 1/2 AB. Correct.\n\n4) Two secants intersecting inside: P is inside. The two intercepted arcs are, say, arc AB and arc CD. Angle is 1/2 (AB + CD). Correct.\n\n5) Tangent and secant outside: P is outside. The two intercepted arcs are the arc between the tangent point and the secant's intersection points. The angle is 1/2 (larger arc - smaller arc). Correct.\n\n6) Two secants outside: Similarly, angle is 1/2 (difference of arcs). Correct.\n\n7) Circumscribed angle (two tangents): P is outside. The angle is 1/2 (difference of the intercepted arcs). Correct.\n\nTherefore, this general formulation seems to encompass all seven theorems as special cases based on the position of point P (inside, on, or outside the circle) and accordingly using sum, single arc, or difference.\n\nThus, the common formulation can be succinctly stated as:\n\nThe measure of an angle formed by two lines intersecting at a point P relative to a circle is half the sum of the intercepted arcs if P is inside the circle, half the measure of the intercepted arc if P is on the circle, and half the difference of the intercepted arcs if P is outside the circle.\n\nAlternatively, to make it even more concise:\n\nThe measure of an angle formed by two intersecting lines with vertex P relative to a circle is equal to:\n\n- \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\) times the sum of the measures of the intercepted arcs when P is inside the circle,\n\n- \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\) times the measure of the intercepted arc when P is on the circle,\n\n- \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\) times the difference of the measures of the intercepted arcs when P is outside the circle.\n\nThis formulation covers all seven cases by considering the position of the vertex and adjusting the formula accordingly. Each specific theorem is derived based on where the vertex is located and whether the lines are secants, tangents, or chords.\n\nI need to ensure that this formulation correctly accounts for all cases. Let's check the tangent-secant outside case (theorem 5): vertex outside, so half the difference of the intercepted arcs. The tangent touches at one point, and the secant intersects the circle at two points. The intercepted arcs would be the arc between the tangent point and the far secant point, and the arc between the two secant points. The angle is half the difference of these arcs. Yes, that matches theorem 5.\n\nSimilarly, for two tangents (theorem 7), the vertex is outside, and the intercepted arcs are the two arcs between the points of tangency. The angle is half the difference (major arc - minor arc). Correct.\n\nTherefore, this general statement seems to hold. The key was recognizing that the position of the vertex dictates whether we use sum, single, or difference of arcs, all scaled by 1/2. The central angle is a special case where the sum of the intercepted arcs (which are the same) gives twice the arc, hence half the sum is the arc measure itself.", "deepseek_solution": "The measure of an angle formed by two intersecting lines relative to a circle can be universally described by the following general principle, which subsumes all seven theorems as special cases:\n\n**General Formulation:**  \nThe measure of an angle with vertex \\( P \\) formed by two lines intersecting the circle is determined by the position of \\( P \\):  \n- **Inside the circle:** \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{(sum of intercepted arcs)} \\),  \n- **On the circle:** \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{(single intercepted arc)} \\),  \n- **Outside the circle:** \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{(difference of intercepted arcs)} \\).  \n\n### Derivation of Specific Theorems:\n1. **Central Angle (Vertex at Center):**  \n   - \\( P \\) is inside, and the intercepted arcs are equal (arc \\( \\equiv \\) arc).  \n   - Angle \\( = \\frac{1}{2}(arc + arc) = arc \\).  \n\n2. **Inscribed Angle (Vertex on Circle):**  \n   - \\( P \\) is on the circle.  \n   - Angle \\( = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{intercepted arc} \\).  \n\n3. **Tangent-Chord Angle (Vertex on Circle):**  \n   - Similar to inscribed angle; vertex on the circle.  \n   - Angle \\( = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{intercepted arc} \\).  \n\n4. **Two Secants Intersecting Inside:**  \n   - \\( P \\) is inside.  \n   - Angle \\( = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{(sum of intercepted arcs)} \\).  \n\n5. **Tangent-Secant Outside:**  \n   - \\( P \\) is outside.  \n   - Angle \\( = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{(difference of intercepted arcs)} \\).  \n\n6. **Two Secants Outside:**  \n   - \\( P \\) is outside.  \n   - Angle \\( = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{(difference of intercepted arcs)} \\).  \n\n7. **Circumscribed Angle (Two Tangents):**  \n   - \\( P \\) is outside.  \n   - Angle \\( = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{(difference of intercepted arcs)} \\).  \n\n### Final Answer:\nAll seven theorems are unified under the general rule:  \n**The measure of an angle formed by two lines intersecting at a point \\( P \\) relative to a circle is**  \n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\frac{1}{2}(\\text{sum of intercepted arcs}) & \\text{if } P \\text{ is inside}, \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(\\text{intercepted arc}) & \\text{if } P \\text{ is on the circle}, \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}(\\text{difference of intercepted arcs}) & \\text{if } P \\text{ is outside}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]  \nEach theorem arises as a special case based on \\( P \\)'s location.", "ground_truth_solution": "To provide a comprehensive formulation that encompasses the seven theorems given, we start by examining the relationship between angles formed by secants and tangents (or their combinations) with the circle and the arcs they intercept. We can synthesize these relationships into one general statement.\n\n1. **Central Angle Theorem**: The measure of a central angle is equal to the measure of the arc it intercepts. \n\n\\[ \\angle AOB = \\text{Arc } AB \\]\n\n2. **Inscribed Angle Theorem**: The measure of an inscribed angle is half the measure of the arc it intercepts.\n\n\\[ \\angle ACB = \\frac{1}{2} \\text{Arc } AB \\]\n\n3. **Angle Formed by a Tangent and a Chord Theorem**: The measure of an angle formed by a tangent and a chord through the point of tangency is half the measure of its intercepted arc.\n\n\\[ \\angle ATB = \\frac{1}{2} \\text{Arc } ACB \\]\n\n4. **Angle formed by Two Intersecting Chords (or Secants) Theorem**: The measure of an angle formed by two intersecting chords inside a circle is half the sum of the measures of the arcs intercepted by the angle and its vertical angle.\n\n\\[ \\angle AED = \\frac{1}{2} (\\text{Arc } AB + \\text{Arc } CD) \\]\n\n5. **Angle Formed by a Secant and a Tangent (External) Theorem**: The measure of an angle formed by a secant and a tangent outside a circle is half the difference of the measures of the intercepted arcs.\n\n\\[ \\angle BCD = \\frac{1}{2} (\\text{Arc } BD - \\text{Arc } AC) \\]\n\n6. **Angle Formed by Two Intersecting Secants Outside a Circle Theorem**: The measure of an angle formed by two secants intersecting outside a circle is half the difference of the measures of the intercepted arcs.\n\n\\[ \\angle AEC = \\frac{1}{2} (\\text{Arc } AC - \\text{Arc } BD) \\]\n\n7. **Angle Inscribed in a Semicircle (Special Case of Inscribed Angle)**: When the vertex of an angle is on the circumference of a circle, and the angle intercepts a semicircle, then the angle is a right angle.\n\nNow, we combine all these cases into one general theorem formulation:\n\n**General Formulation**:\n\"The measure of an angle, where the sides are secants or tangents of a given circle, is given by the half-sum or half-difference of the measures of the arcs between its sides, depending on whether the vertex of the angle is inside the circle or outside it. When the vertex lies on the circle, one of the arcs contributes zero to the measurement.\"\n\nThis general statement can be mathematically expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{If } \\angle ABC \\text{ is formed by two secants or tangents, define:}\n\\]\n\n- **Inside Circle**: \n\\[ \\angle ABC = \\frac{1}{2}(\\text{Arc } DE + \\text{Arc } FG) \\]\n\n- **Outside Circle**:\n\\[ \\angle ABC = \\frac{1}{2} (\\text{Arc } DE - \\text{Arc } FG) \\]\n\n- **On the Circle**: (one arc is zero)\n\\[ \\angle ABC = \\frac{1}{2}(\\text{Arc } DE) \\]\n\nAs a conclusion, this generalized theorem gracefully encapsulates all the specific cases mentioned. Hence, the final unified theorem is boxed as:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The measure of an angle, where the sides are secants or tangents of a given circle, is determined by the half-sum or half-difference of the intercepted arcs' measures, respecting whether the vertex is interior or exterior to the circle; while when on the circle, one of the arcs is considered zero.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given that \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) are two subsets of the set \\(\\{1,2,3, \\cdots, 100\\}\\), satisfying \\( |A| = |B| \\) and \\( A \\cap B = \\varnothing \\). If \\( x \\in A \\) always implies \\( 2x + 2 \\in B \\), what is the maximum number of elements in the set \\( A \\cup B \\)?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's try to tackle this problem. So, we have two subsets A and B of the set {1, 2, 3, ..., 100}. They have the same size, and they're disjoint. Also, if an element x is in A, then 2x + 2 must be in B. We need to find the maximum number of elements in A union B. Hmm, interesting.\n\nFirst, let's parse the problem again to make sure I understand all the constraints. A and B are subsets of {1, ..., 100}, they're the same size, disjoint, and for every x in A, 2x + 2 is in B. We need to maximize |A \u222a B|. Since A and B are disjoint, |A \u222a B| = |A| + |B| = 2|A| because |A| = |B|. So, maximizing |A \u222a B| is equivalent to maximizing |A|, given the constraints.\n\nSo, the key is to find the largest possible size of A such that for every x in A, 2x + 2 is in B, and A and B are disjoint. Since B is just the set of elements of the form 2x + 2 where x is in A, but also B has to be the same size as A. Wait, but if every x in A maps to 2x + 2 in B, then B is exactly {2x + 2 | x \u2208 A}, right? But since A and B are disjoint, none of the elements in B can be in A. So, for any x in A, 2x + 2 cannot be in A. Also, since |B| = |A|, the mapping from A to B must be injective. But since x is unique, 2x + 2 is unique for each x, so the mapping is injective. But we also need to ensure that 2x + 2 is within the set {1, ..., 100}; otherwise, it's not in B. So, 2x + 2 \u2264 100, which means x \u2264 49. So, elements in A can't be larger than 49. Wait, but 2*49 + 2 = 100, which is still within 100. So, x can be at most 49. Therefore, the possible elements of A are from 1 to 49. But not necessarily all of them, because we need to make sure that 2x + 2 is in B and B is disjoint from A. So, if x is in A, then 2x + 2 can't be in A. So, for all x in A, 2x + 2 \u2209 A. Therefore, A must not contain any elements of the form 2y + 2 where y is in A. So, there's a kind of recursive constraint here. \n\nAlternatively, maybe we can model this as a graph where each element x in {1, ..., 49} is connected to 2x + 2, and we need to choose a set A such that none of the elements in A are connected to each other, and B is the image of A under this mapping. Then, the problem reduces to finding the 